### 14\. Snort 3 command-line options

These are commands and configuration switches rather than rule filters.

| Command              | Function                            |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| snort -V             | Displays Snort version              |
| snort -h             | Displays help                       |
| snort -T             | Tests configuration                 |
| snort -c             | Specifies configuration file        |
| snort -i             | Selects network interface           |
| snort -r             | Reads a PCAP file                   |
| snort -R             | Loads a rule file                   |
| snort -A             | Selects alert output mode           |
| snort -l             | Specifies log directory             |
| snort -q             | Quiet mode                          |
| snort -D             | Runs in daemon mode where supported |
| snort --daq-list     | Lists available DAQ modules         |
| snort --help-options | Displays available options          |

Example:

```bash
sudo snort -T -c /etc/snort/snort.lua
```

This tests the Snort configuration.

Capture traffic on an Ubuntu interface:

```bash
sudo snort -c /etc/snort/snort.lua -i eth0
```

Read an existing packet capture:

```bash
sudo snort -c /etc/snort/snort.lua -r traffic.pcap
```

The interface and configuration paths may differ on your installation.

### 15\. Practical Snort rules for your Ubuntu laboratory

Here are five basic rules you can practise with on a network you own or are authorised to monitor.

A. Detect ping traffic

```snort
alert icmp any any -> any any (msg:"ICMP traffic detected"; sid:1000100; rev:1;)
```

B. Detect DNS traffic

```snort
alert udp any any -> any 53 (msg:"DNS traffic detected"; sid:1000101; rev:1;)
```

C. Detect SSH connection attempts

```snort
alert tcp any any -> any 22 (msg:"SSH connection detected"; sid:1000102; rev:1;)
```

D. Detect HTTP requests

```snort
alert http any any -> any any (msg:"HTTP request detected"; flow:to_server; sid:1000103; rev:1;)
```

E. Detect repeated connections to a service

```snort
alert tcp any any -> any 443
(msg:"Repeated HTTPS connection activity";
 detection_filter:track by_src,count 20,seconds 60;
 sid:1000104;
 rev:1;)
```

These are introductory monitoring rules, not proof of malicious behaviour.

